WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.362
Unit 1 is the shape of the service.

2
00:00:02.362 --> 00:00:06.642
Defender X D R joins Defender for
Endpoint, Defender for Identity,

3
00:00:06.642 --> 00:00:09.971
Defender for Office three
sixty-five and Defender

4
00:00:09.971 --> 00:00:13.300
for Cloud Apps in one portal
and one incident queue.

5
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.100
Two trust boundaries matter.

6
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:21.025
The agency's boundary holds the devices,
the S O C's tools and the evidence store.

7
00:00:21.025 --> 00:00:22.987
Microsoft's holds the service.

8
00:00:22.987 --> 00:00:28.982
Microsoft Learn's page on Defender for U S
Government says that for a U S Government

9
00:00:28.982 --> 00:00:33.124
tenant it's built in the Azure
U S Government environment,

10
00:00:33.124 --> 00:00:36.738
and G C C, G C C High and D O
D each have their own portal.

11
00:00:36.738 --> 00:00:38.299
Then the control planes.

12
00:00:38.299 --> 00:00:43.092
Learn says Intune is the recommended tool
for distributing Defender for Endpoint

13
00:00:43.092 --> 00:00:46.565
settings, and that its policies
apply to device objects only,

14
00:00:46.565 --> 00:00:50.038
so they're assigned to Entra
device groups, never user groups.

15
00:00:50.038 --> 00:00:53.500
Settings Intune doesn't
hold, like automation levels

16
00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:56.726
and custom detections, live
in the Defender portal.

17
00:00:56.726 --> 00:01:02.295
Servers in Azure, A W S, Google Cloud or
Oracle Cloud Infrastructure reach Defender

18
00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:05.244
through Defender for
Servers and Azure Arc,

19
00:01:05.244 --> 00:01:08.275
as the Devices and
Endpoints book describes.

20
00:01:08.275 --> 00:01:11.756
And one rule above all: one
control plane per setting,

21
00:01:11.756 --> 00:01:15.140
because Group Policy wins
over Intune by default.
